                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the capability gap, not the brief's label`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Continentale's brief is one page: <em>"our managers manage operations well but cannot lead transformation."</em> That is the <strong>presenting</strong> problem, not the <strong>underlying</strong> gap. Your discovery pack — six stakeholder interviews, an engagement-survey extract and talent-review data on the 300 managers — points elsewhere: managers <strong>avoid cross-silo conflict</strong> and <strong>freeze on transformation trade-offs</strong>. This is Gallet's craft — translate a vague ambition into a measurable behaviour-change journey. The trap is designing a "strategic leadership" course because the label said so. Name the real gap, choose 3–4 target behaviours, and decide what proves change at 6 months.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the learning solution & faculty mix`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Architect a coherent <strong>blended journey</strong> (70-20-10), not a list of sessions. Each format trades off: star academics drive perceived value but only <strong>24 days exist</strong> between the two; asynchronous digital scales cheaply but transfers behaviour weakly alone; an <strong>action-learning project</strong> lifts transfer-to-work but costs build time. Allocate faculty days across scarce stars, scalable affiliates and client executives — and over-promise the marquee names and you cannot deliver across ten cohorts. Build in the mechanism that actually changes behaviour <em>on the job</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Price under the margin & launch squeeze`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Build the engagement P&L. Price against the <strong>€1.8M cap</strong> while protecting the <strong>35% contribution margin</strong> (≈€630k). Drop the price to undercut the consultancy and you sink below the floor — three programs already ran below it last year. Then absorb the <strong>16-week launch</strong> the board wants when design normally takes 22–26 weeks: a phased MVP cohort, priced overtime, or a quiet quality cut? And take a clear position on the <strong>20% (€360k) impact-linked fee</strong> tied to behaviour change. The discovery: relevance, margin, faculty and speed cannot all be maxed — <em>the winning bid makes the trade-offs explicit and defensible</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Pitch to the client sponsor`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Three minutes to the CHRO and a procurement lead. Lead with the <strong>diagnosis</strong>, show why your journey changes the named behaviours, prove the faculty mix is <em>deliverable, not over-promised</em>, justify the price, and commit to measuring impact at 6 months. Then handle two live objections: <strong>"the consultancy is 15% cheaper"</strong> and <strong>"can you really launch in 16 weeks?"</strong> Defend value and feasibility — discount the margin away to win and you set a loss-making precedent your CFO will not tolerate, and you weaken the wider €3.1M relationship.`,

                    r1_gap_label:           `The brief says "our managers manage operations well but cannot lead transformation." What is the real gap?`,
                    r1_evidence_label:      `Which discovery source do you anchor the diagnosis on?`,
                    r1_behaviours_label:    `How many concrete, measurable target behaviours will the program change?`,
                    r1_behaviours_hint:     `Gallet's discipline: 3–4 named behaviours with impact metrics. Too few is vague; too many is unfocused.`,
