                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_gap_right:     `<strong>That is the diagnosed gap, not the brief's label.</strong> The transcripts say managers avoid cross-silo conflict and freeze on transformation trade-offs — an influence-and-courage problem, not a strategy-frameworks problem. Now you can design something that actually moves the strategy.`,
                    coach_r1_brief:         `<strong>You built a strategy course because the brief said "strategic."</strong> But the CHRO's transcript describes managers who avoid cross-silo conflict and freeze on trade-offs. You have designed a strategy course for an influence-and-courage problem. What in your discovery pack told you the real gap — and did you read it?`,
                    coach_r1_talent:        `<strong>Blaming the talent dodges the design problem.</strong> The client commissioned a program to change behaviour, not a verdict that its 300 managers are the wrong people. Diagnose the behaviour gap your journey can move.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the underlying behaviour gap AND triangulated it across interviews, the engagement survey and talent data. That cross-checked diagnosis is exactly what separates a custom design from a catalogue best-of — and it is what you will defend in the pitch.`,
                    coach_r1_evbrief:       `<strong>Anchoring on the one-pager is how you solve the wrong problem.</strong> The client's own label is the presenting symptom. Your discovery pack exists precisely because the brief cannot be trusted as the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r1_toofew:        `<strong>One vague behaviour is not a design brief.</strong> "Be more strategic" cannot be measured or built backward from. Name 3–4 concrete behaviours the program will change, or the impact fee is unwinnable.`,
                    coach_r1_toomany:       `<strong>Seven-plus target behaviours is an unfocused wish list.</strong> A 300-manager program that tries to change everything changes nothing. Gallet's discipline is 3–4 named behaviours with metrics.`,
                    coach_r1_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction is not impact.</strong> Happy sheets prove people enjoyed it, not that behaviour changed. With €360k tied to 6-month transfer, design backward from 360 uplift and a transfer-to-work index from the start.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>A blended journey is the right spine.</strong> In-person intensives for the hard conversations, virtual and digital to scale, coaching and an action project to transfer it on the job — that is 70-20-10, not a stack of lectures.`,
                    coach_r2_alllive:       `<strong>Beautiful and unaffordable.</strong> A fully bespoke, all-in-person journey is the most relevant — and it blows the €1.8M cap and the 35% margin once you cost the faculty days. Which components must be live, and which can scale digitally without losing the behaviour change?`,
                    coach_r2_digital:       `<strong>Cheap and scalable, but it does not transfer alone.</strong> Asynchronous digital is efficient for content, but the influence-and-courage behaviours change through practice, coaching and live challenge — not modules watched at a desk.`,
                    coach_r2_lecture:       `<strong>A lecture series is a catalogue answer to a custom brief.</strong> It is simple to build and exactly what a consultancy could undercut. Generic content will not change cross-silo influence behaviour.`,
                    coach_r2_overpromise:   `<strong>You have promised faculty you do not have.</strong> Your design needs more than the 24 star-days that exist between the two academics the client trusts. Either you backfill with affiliates the client never approved, or the names on the brochure are not in the room in week six. Design to real capacity.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A deliverable mix.</strong> Enough star credibility to anchor the brand, scalable affiliates to carry the volume across ten cohorts, and client executives lifting relevance and ownership — all within the days that actually exist. That is staffing to capacity, not to the bid.`,
                    coach_r2_nostar:        `<strong>No star faculty at all undercuts the perceived value.</strong> The client knows two names and expects to see them. A little of their scarce time anchors credibility; just do not over-promise it across every cohort.`,
                    coach_r2_transfer:      `<strong>The action-learning project is your transfer engine.</strong> A live business challenge with manager involvement and spaced practice is where behaviour changes on the job — and it is what makes the €360k impact fee defensible rather than a gamble.`,
                    coach_r2_content:       `<strong>More content is not more transfer.</strong> Adding knowledge modules assumes behaviour follows information. It rarely does — the influence-and-courage gap closes through practice, not extra slides.`,
                    coach_r2_notransfer:    `<strong>No transfer mechanism is a satisfaction bet.</strong> A strong classroom experience changes reaction and learning, not behaviour at six months. With the impact fee on the line, you have designed nothing that moves transfer-to-work.`,
                    coach_r3_phased:        `<strong>A phased MVP is the disciplined way to hit 16 weeks.</strong> Launch cohort one with what is genuinely ready and build the rest in parallel — the board sees a live cohort in week sixteen without you quietly cutting the quality of the whole program.`,
                    coach_r3_overtime:      `<strong>Priced overtime is honest, if expensive.</strong> Compressing the full 22-week build into 16 weeks costs real money — at least you have put it in the P&L rather than burning your team and pretending it was free.`,
                    coach_r3_sayyes:        `<strong>You said yes to 16 weeks with no plan for the six weeks.</strong> Design normally takes 22. Where do they come from — unpriced overtime, a phased cohort, or a quality you will quietly cut? The board sees cohort one in week sixteen; what exactly is ready?`,
                    coach_r3_pushback:      `<strong>Refusing the timeline outright loses the bid.</strong> The board's week-16 launch is a hard constraint, not an opening position. A blanket "it needs 24 weeks" hands the relevance argument to the consultancy. Find the phased path instead.`,
                    coach_r3_overcap:       `<strong>You have breached the €1.8M cap.</strong> Relevance is not free, but the client budget is fixed. Price above the cap and procurement screens you out before the CHRO ever hears the diagnosis. Bring the design back inside the cap.`,
                    coach_r3_belowfloor:    `<strong>You are below the 35% margin floor.</strong> Your CFO set that floor because three programs already ran under it last year and diluted the portfolio. You may win the deal, but did you win the business? Defend the price on relevance, do not discount it.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>Margin-disciplined and inside the cap.</strong> You protected the 35% floor, stayed under €1.8M, and chose a phased launch you can actually deliver. That is a bid your CFO signs and your client believes — relevance defended, not discounted.`,
                    coach_r3_impactdesign:  `<strong>Designing the measurement in is the right call on the impact fee.</strong> You are betting €360k on the transfer mechanism you actually built, not on goodwill — which is exactly the discipline that makes the fee a confident commitment rather than a gamble.`,
                    coach_r3_satisfaction:  `<strong>You are comfortable with the impact fee because people rate you highly.</strong> But €360k is tied to 360 uplift and transfer-to-work at six months, not happy sheets. Where in your design does behaviour change on the job — or are you gambling €360k on goodwill?`,
                    coach_r3_rejectfee:     `<strong>Refusing the impact structure signals you do not back your own design.</strong> A client buying behaviour change wants you to share the risk. If your journey really transfers, accept the fee and design the measurement in — do not run from it.`,
                    coach_r3_scopecreep:    `<strong>Loose change-control is how the margin quietly dies.</strong> Three programs ran below the floor last year because scope ballooned after signature. Without firm change-control, the bespoke extras the client requests in week eight come straight out of your 35%.`,
                    coach_r4_diagnosis:     `<strong>Leading with the diagnosis is exactly right.</strong> Open with the gap you named and the behaviours your journey changes, and the price conversation becomes about value, not cost. That is how a custom division beats a cheaper consultancy.`,
                    coach_r4_names:         `<strong>Leading with names and rankings is a catalogue pitch.</strong> The client can get brand and stars from any top school. What it cannot get elsewhere is your diagnosis of its specific gap — lead with that, not the brochure.`,
                    coach_r4_leadprice:     `<strong>Leading with price concedes the whole game.</strong> The moment the pitch is about how competitive your fee is, you are on the consultancy's turf and the diagnosis you did never gets heard. Lead with relevance.`,
                    coach_r4_holdvalue:     `<strong>That is how you beat a cheaper bid.</strong> You held the price and answered the 15% gap with diagnosed relevance and measured transfer — value the consultancy cannot match. The CHRO is buying behaviour change, not the lowest number.`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>You dropped €250k to undercut the consultancy.</strong> You are now below the 35% floor your CFO defends for a reason. You won the deal; did you win the business? Below-floor deals set a precedent — and the consultancy will just cut again next time.`,
                    coach_r4_trim:          `<strong>Quietly trimming a module to lower the price erodes the diagnosis.</strong> The first thing to go is usually the transfer mechanism — and now you are defending a cheaper program that changes less behaviour, with the impact fee still on the line.`,
                    coach_r4_promise:       `<strong>Promising everything in 16 weeks is the trap.</strong> You have just committed to a full build in a compressed window with no phased plan. In week six, when it is not ready, the client remembers this answer. Show them what is actually ready by week sixteen.`,
                    coach_r4_renegotiate:   `<strong>Re-opening the timeline at the pitch destroys trust.</strong> Telling the CHRO only now that it really needs 24 weeks reads as a bait-and-switch. The time to plan the compression was the design, not the room.`,
                    coach_r4_strongclose:   `<strong>A winning close.</strong> You led with the diagnosis, defended the price on value against the 15% gap, and proved the 16-week launch with a concrete phased plan — relevance, margin, faculty and speed all held and made explicit. That is how the division wins the right way.`,

